<Project>
    <!--
        Declared here because this package owns the verb attributes, and the property that binds a
        trigger has to be declared wherever the attribute that needs it can be written. See
        Hardened.Functions.Runtime's targets for the rule.

        This one is why the rule exists rather than putting all six in one place: a Kestrel or
        ASP.NET Core project writes [Get] without ever referencing Hardened.Functions.Runtime, so a
        declaration living there would leave HardenedHttpModule invisible - the identical failure
        <HardenedAmbiguousRoutes> shipped with.

        All five verbs name one property. A controller with a GET and a POST registers one adapter.
    -->
    <ItemGroup>
        <CompilerVisibleProperty Include="HardenedHttpModule"/>
    </ItemGroup>

    <!--
        A route registered with a lambda is served by rewriting the registration call, and the
        compiler only honours an interceptor in a namespace the project has named. The generator
        emits into $(AssemblyName).Generated, so that is what is named here - an application does
        not have to know the mechanism exists to use the feature.

        Both spellings. InterceptorsPreviewNamespaces is what the 8.0.4xx SDKs read and
        InterceptorsNamespaces is what replaced it; setting a property the compiler does not know
        costs nothing, and setting only one leaves the feature silently off on the other.
    -->
    <PropertyGroup>
        <InterceptorsNamespaces>$(InterceptorsNamespaces);$(AssemblyName).Generated</InterceptorsNamespaces>
        <InterceptorsPreviewNamespaces>$(InterceptorsPreviewNamespaces);$(AssemblyName).Generated</InterceptorsPreviewNamespaces>
    </PropertyGroup>
</Project>
